  "slug": "aging-senescence-a873",
  "summary": "Spatial and single-cell multi-omics now resolve senescence to specific cell types and niches across human and mouse aging tissues.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the resolution of aging and senescence at cell-type and spatial scale. In human ovary, single-cell and spatial multi-omics across menopause identify granulosa-cell remodelling and implicate HEYL in ovarian aging and senescence. In human omental visceral adipose, older adults show more senescent cells than obesity alone explains, with senescent stem/progenitor and mesothelial cells participating in multicellular signaling that impairs organ function and drives inflammation. A multi-omic spinal cord atlas in mice and humans links age-related changes and senescence markers to specific cell types. In aged mouse liver, senescent cells localize to spatial inflammatory niches (SenSpots) with distinct epigenetic senotypes, while p53 loss increases inflammation and metabolic damage and p53 activation by an MDM2 inhibitor is reported to reverse age-linked changes. What is new is the combination of cell-type resolution with spatial context and perturbation-linked evidence, which frames senescence as tissue- and cell-type-specific.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-20"
